1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,715
In this video, I wanted
to add a few tips

2
00:00:02,715 --> 00:00:04,890
beyond just getting
the basic build done

3
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:07,050
to describe some more tips for

4
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:10,350
using Buildroot
for your project.

5
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,380
The learning objectives
here we'll just be

6
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:15,110
giving additional tips
for using Buildroot,

7
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:17,910
and also for completing
assignment Number 4.

8
00:00:17,910 --> 00:00:20,520
First is you might
notice in some of

9
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,980
the scripts that they
start with their name $0.

10
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:27,930
I wanted to describe
what this was.

11
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:31,170
You can use $0 to reference

12
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:32,805
the full path and

13
00:00:32,805 --> 00:00:35,325
name of the executable
that was started.

14
00:00:35,325 --> 00:00:38,190
If you started a script.sh
from some path and

15
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:41,280
you use /path/toscript.sh,

16
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,895
then $0 would have that

17
00:00:44,895 --> 00:00:50,340
/pathtoscript.sh
as the full name

18
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,905
of the path that we executed.

19
00:00:52,905 --> 00:00:55,690
The dirname strips out
just the directory path.

20
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:57,895
That would give us /pathto.

21
00:00:57,895 --> 00:01:02,710
When we do cd `dirname $0,

22
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:04,300
what this is doing
is that we change

23
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:07,265
directory into the path
where the script is located.

24
00:01:07,265 --> 00:01:10,090
This allows us to
ensure that when

25
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:13,030
we're calling a script
from some other path,

26
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:14,590
we always cd into

27
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:16,870
the directory containing the
script before we run it.

28
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:17,920
This way, if we have

29
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,600
other scripts that we're
going to reference,

30
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,090
we can reference that knowing
that we're running from

31
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:25,670
the directory where
the script is located.

32
00:01:25,670 --> 00:01:27,940
I also wanted to talk about

33
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:31,505
single package rebuilds
and local builds.

34
00:01:31,505 --> 00:01:33,630
You might be used to,

35
00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:39,250
as a brute force every time
you make an executable,

36
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:42,635
you do a clean step and
then you do a build step.

37
00:01:42,635 --> 00:01:45,550
Well, it turns out that clean
steps are really painful

38
00:01:45,550 --> 00:01:48,250
when you're building an
entire embedded system.

39
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:49,270
Because if you think about all

40
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:50,440
the different things
that you're building

41
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,225
as a part of a make operation,

42
00:01:53,225 --> 00:01:57,520
it's going to take probably
hours to get the build done

43
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:02,205
depending on the capabilities
of your host system.

44
00:02:02,205 --> 00:02:04,570
You really want to
avoid doing clean

45
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:08,210
builds in terms of
cleaning the entire image.

46
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:11,015
You want to be able to iterate

47
00:02:11,015 --> 00:02:14,705
on changes to an
individual package easily.

48
00:02:14,705 --> 00:02:19,340
There's several different ways
to do this with Buildroot.

49
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:21,575
One is, if you
just want to build

50
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:23,530
a specific package, you can say,

51
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:26,085
make the package name-rebuild

52
00:02:26,085 --> 00:02:28,320
or make the package
name reconfigure,

53
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,775
from the Buildroot directory,

54
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:31,550
in order to do just that

55
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:34,350
build stuff for that
particular package.

56
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:41,390
If you remember in our
package,.makefile,

57
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:44,345
we specify an
individual commit hash

58
00:02:44,345 --> 00:02:46,880
that maps to the source
that we want to use.

59
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:48,410
Well, what happens when you're

60
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:49,820
trying to fix something with

61
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,465
your code and you're making
a whole bunch of changes.

62
00:02:53,465 --> 00:02:55,160
You don't want to have
to push those up to

63
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,320
the Git repository and go in and

64
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:00,235
change that commit hash
over and over again.

65
00:03:00,235 --> 00:03:02,415
You can use local
builds for that.

66
00:03:02,415 --> 00:03:04,835
You can use a make argument

67
00:03:04,835 --> 00:03:07,190
that specifies the
name of your package,

68
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:10,760
and then uses
OVERRIDE_SRCDIR equals,

69
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,270
and then just give it a path
to the local directory.

70
00:03:13,270 --> 00:03:15,210
What this will do is ignore

71
00:03:15,210 --> 00:03:18,105
the Git URL that you
specified for this build,

72
00:03:18,105 --> 00:03:20,660
and instead, find
the source code at

73
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:23,360
the location that you specified
here at pathto local.

74
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:29,820
You combine the AESD assignments

75
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:32,070
override SRCDIR with the make

76
00:03:32,070 --> 00:03:33,630
AESD assignments rebuild or

77
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:35,670
make AESD assignments
reconfigure,

78
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:37,190
to iterate really quickly on

79
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:39,800
changes that you have
to your local source.

80
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:43,310
I encourage you to
read about these,

81
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:45,845
and test the model
with this assignment,

82
00:03:45,845 --> 00:03:47,375
get used to using them.

83
00:03:47,375 --> 00:03:50,525
Because as we get more
complicated assignments later,

84
00:03:50,525 --> 00:03:51,740
it's going to be
important that you

85
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,240
use this as part
of your workflow.

86
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,590
Whatever you do, do not

87
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:56,090
do a clean build
every time you make

88
00:03:56,090 --> 00:03:57,830
a change because
it's going to take

89
00:03:57,830 --> 00:03:59,735
you forever to get
the assignment done,

90
00:03:59,735 --> 00:04:02,160
waiting for your
builds to complete.

91
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:05,475
The other thing I mentioned,

92
00:04:05,475 --> 00:04:08,010
the builds are going to take
a long time to complete.

93
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:10,240
You're probably
half-hour to hour

94
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,620
range depending on the
capabilities of your hardware.

95
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:14,320
What if, if you're using

96
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:17,260
SSH as a part of
your workflow and

97
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:23,020
your screen session dies or
is closed for some reason,

98
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,700
like for instance, maybe if

99
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,880
you lost network
connectivity or something,

100
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:30,700
what would happen to your build?

101
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:33,125
Your build actually
stops in that case.

102
00:04:33,125 --> 00:04:36,340
If you are using SSH as part
of your workflow and you're

103
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:39,760
SSHing into the system that
you're using for build,

104
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,860
one alternative is
that you can have

105
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,785
a dedicated screen
session for your Build.

106
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:46,090
You can use this like this,

107
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:48,730
you can define a
build screen session,

108
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,750
then you can start the build
from within the session,

109
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:53,185
and then you can use
Control ID to disconnect.

110
00:04:53,185 --> 00:04:55,410
To reconnect you
can use screen-r.

111
00:04:55,410 --> 00:04:59,150
This just helps keep
long-running tasks happening in

112
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:03,685
the background and not end
on you and your SSH session.

113
00:05:03,685 --> 00:05:05,600
You can read more about
the screen command

114
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,280
in the link that
I provided here.

115
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:12,275
Another thing that
I'll provide as a tip,

116
00:05:12,275 --> 00:05:15,110
I mentioned before that we
need to use the SSH URL,

117
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:16,370
based on the fact
that we want to

118
00:05:16,370 --> 00:05:17,720
automate the build and we

119
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:21,035
are using a private repository,

120
00:05:21,035 --> 00:05:22,460
which is typically
probably going to be

121
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:25,705
the case for your
production builds as well.

122
00:05:25,705 --> 00:05:28,180
If you kick off a normal build,

123
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:30,080
there's a bunch of work
that the builds are

124
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,060
going to do first
install a toolchain.

125
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:33,830
It'll be quite a while
before it actually gets to

126
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:36,685
the point that it needs
to compile your package.

127
00:05:36,685 --> 00:05:38,765
When it gets to that point,

128
00:05:38,765 --> 00:05:40,475
that needs to compile
your package,

129
00:05:40,475 --> 00:05:45,890
if you have a password
associated with your SSH key,

130
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:47,810
then it's going to stall
there waiting for you to

131
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:50,335
enter your key so
that it can continue.

132
00:05:50,335 --> 00:05:53,899
What I suggest instead is
either use a passwordless

133
00:05:53,899 --> 00:05:57,410
key or use SSH agent in
your terminal session.

134
00:05:57,410 --> 00:06:00,605
In the same window that
you're using to do the build,

135
00:06:00,605 --> 00:06:03,260
you'd run these commands,
eval SSH agent,

136
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:05,810
and then SSH add with
a path to your key.

137
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:07,400
It'll ask you for your key

138
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:09,050
and you'll enter your
key at that time.

139
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:11,045
Then, after that point,

140
00:06:11,045 --> 00:06:13,490
any of the SSH-related commands

141
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:15,950
that happen after that
will automatically use

142
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:18,290
your key and the credentials
that you've already stored

143
00:06:18,290 --> 00:06:21,810
in that particular
terminal session.

144
00:06:21,810 --> 00:06:24,385
Another thing I
wanted to mention,

145
00:06:24,385 --> 00:06:26,260
students have had
weird errors in

146
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:28,780
the past because
they've used spaces

147
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:30,130
in paths and you can get

148
00:06:30,130 --> 00:06:31,480
some really confusing
errors out of

149
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:33,295
Buildroot if you do this.

150
00:06:33,295 --> 00:06:35,710
This is not something
that's typically done.

151
00:06:35,710 --> 00:06:37,255
If you're coming from Windows,

152
00:06:37,255 --> 00:06:40,300
this might be more normal
to you to have a space in

153
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:44,560
a path or in a directory
name for instance.

154
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:47,560
That's not a very
common thing on Linux,

155
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:48,745
and it's not something that's

156
00:06:48,745 --> 00:06:51,640
well-supported by any
tools, including Buildroot.

157
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,300
Please avoid doing that
and don't use a space,

158
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:57,280
use an underscore or
a hyphen or something

159
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:59,260
like that if you need

160
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:04,310
to separate words in a
file or directory path.

161
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:08,244
The other thing I
wanted to mention,

162
00:07:08,244 --> 00:07:10,150
we've talked about SSH already

163
00:07:10,150 --> 00:07:12,610
and some of you might
be using SSH as part

164
00:07:12,610 --> 00:07:17,665
of your workflow for accessing
a Linux system for builds.

165
00:07:17,665 --> 00:07:22,060
I wanted to talk about using
SSH passthrough for QEMU.

166
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:23,995
Starting with Assignment 4,

167
00:07:23,995 --> 00:07:27,325
we'll use SSH to interact
with our QEMU system.

168
00:07:27,325 --> 00:07:30,670
With Assignment 3 we had a
really simple environment

169
00:07:30,670 --> 00:07:32,320
where we just booted
right into a terminal and

170
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:34,180
we interacted through
that terminal.

171
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:37,615
With Assignments 4 and later
we're going to set up SSH,

172
00:07:37,615 --> 00:07:41,245
since Buildroot makes
that easy for us to do.

173
00:07:41,245 --> 00:07:45,890
Then we're going to SSH
into our QEMU instance.

174
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:50,680
The way that we're going to
start our QEMU instance,

175
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:53,980
we're going to use a
version of networking that

176
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:58,580
essentially shares networking
with the host system.

177
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:03,310
If you're using a Linux
system and you're

178
00:08:03,310 --> 00:08:07,885
using SSH communication
over TCP port 22,

179
00:08:07,885 --> 00:08:10,465
your QEMU is not going to
be able to use that port

180
00:08:10,465 --> 00:08:13,720
because that's already in
use by the host system.

181
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:15,370
We need to modify

182
00:08:15,370 --> 00:08:22,660
our QEMU start script to
forward port a random port

183
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:26,365
to our QEMU port 22 inside

184
00:08:26,365 --> 00:08:32,185
the virtual machine or
inside the test image.

185
00:08:32,185 --> 00:08:36,070
We've picked 10022 for that.

186
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:37,840
You can pick any
number that you want.

187
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:40,315
This is just going to be
the standard for the class.

188
00:08:40,315 --> 00:08:42,490
The important thing to know
about this is that when

189
00:08:42,490 --> 00:08:44,515
you're using SSH to interact,

190
00:08:44,515 --> 00:08:46,930
you're going to use
the -p argument

191
00:08:46,930 --> 00:08:49,135
for SSH to specify
that port number.

192
00:08:49,135 --> 00:08:51,430
That's going to map
to port 22 inside

193
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:54,445
your embedded QEMU instance.

194
00:08:54,445 --> 00:08:56,935
This is another
important thing to note,

195
00:08:56,935 --> 00:08:59,050
I'm not sure why it
works this way but

196
00:08:59,050 --> 00:09:00,970
the scp command that

197
00:09:00,970 --> 00:09:03,130
you'd use for copying
files in and out,

198
00:09:03,130 --> 00:09:04,510
for that one you actually use

199
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:07,390
a P instead of a
lowercase p. You can

200
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:11,605
get some confusing error message
if you use a p with scp.

201
00:09:11,605 --> 00:09:14,420
I just want to make
you aware of this.

202
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:18,070
I mentioned before that
you don't want to just do

203
00:09:18,070 --> 00:09:20,650
clean builds by default
with Buildroot.

204
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:23,499
However, sometimes
it is necessary

205
00:09:23,499 --> 00:09:26,230
to do a clean build
for Buildroot.

206
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:29,410
This is one of the ways
that Buildroot can be a

207
00:09:29,410 --> 00:09:32,530
little bit more confusing
than a tool like Akta.

208
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:34,330
If you read through
the Buildroot manual,

209
00:09:34,330 --> 00:09:36,220
what it says is that it's
the responsibility of

210
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:38,905
the user to know when a
full rebuild is necessary.

211
00:09:38,905 --> 00:09:40,900
Then it gives some examples of

212
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:43,225
when a full rebuild
is necessary.

213
00:09:43,225 --> 00:09:45,010
One is when a target

214
00:09:45,010 --> 00:09:47,860
architecture or toolchain
configuration change.

215
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:49,120
If you ever change

216
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:51,280
something fundamental
like the toolchain,

217
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:52,765
if we switched from

218
00:09:52,765 --> 00:09:55,315
glibc to musl or
something like that,

219
00:09:55,315 --> 00:09:57,790
that would be a case

220
00:09:57,790 --> 00:10:00,130
where you definitely
need a full rebuild.

221
00:10:00,130 --> 00:10:02,260
If you remove a
package in order to

222
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:04,810
actually remove that package
from the root file system,

223
00:10:04,810 --> 00:10:06,940
you can either delete it
out of the root file system

224
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:09,625
yourself or you have
to do a full rebuilt.

225
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:10,870
This part can be a little bit

226
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:13,540
confusing to people
starting out.

227
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:17,380
Sometimes when packages are

228
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:19,104
added that reference
other packages,

229
00:10:19,104 --> 00:10:20,620
you can get into
a scenario where

230
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:22,285
you need to do a full rebuild.

231
00:10:22,285 --> 00:10:24,520
It's more complicated
scenarios than what

232
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:26,545
we're going to get with
this first assignment,

233
00:10:26,545 --> 00:10:29,845
but wanted to let you know
that this was coming up.

234
00:10:29,845 --> 00:10:32,380
If you're changing options on

235
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:35,050
a package that's already
included in the image,

236
00:10:35,050 --> 00:10:36,700
this can be a case
where you need to

237
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:38,260
do a full rebuild to pick

238
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:39,640
those changes up or you need to

239
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:42,340
rebuild that individual package.

240
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:47,190
I bring this all
up because running

241
00:10:47,190 --> 00:10:49,890
a clean step and then
running build usually is

242
00:10:49,890 --> 00:10:51,300
actually a troubleshooting step

243
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:52,665
that you'll want to
use with Buildroot.

244
00:10:52,665 --> 00:10:54,285
Especially if you've
done anything

245
00:10:54,285 --> 00:10:57,410
that might have been
something in this list.

246
00:10:57,410 --> 00:10:59,050
But you definitely don't want

247
00:10:59,050 --> 00:11:00,370
to use this for
incremental changes.

248
00:11:00,370 --> 00:11:02,845
If all you're changing is
the content of your package,

249
00:11:02,845 --> 00:11:04,750
then a clean build is
not going to help you

250
00:11:04,750 --> 00:11:07,610
and it's going to take a
long time to complete.

251
00:11:08,490 --> 00:11:11,050
We already mentioned
the single package

252
00:11:11,050 --> 00:11:13,195
rebuild and local
Build instructions.

253
00:11:13,195 --> 00:11:14,860
That's what you should
be using when you're

254
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:16,750
iterating our changes
to your package.

255
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:19,375
But if you change something
globally in configuration,

256
00:11:19,375 --> 00:11:21,340
I just want to make you
aware that there are times

257
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:24,415
you will need to do a
clean build in that case.

258
00:11:24,415 --> 00:11:28,060
I mentioned that you could
manually remove the content

259
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:31,795
from the root file system.

260
00:11:31,795 --> 00:11:34,855
This is one handy
thing about Buildroot.

261
00:11:34,855 --> 00:11:36,865
There's this Buildroot
output directory

262
00:11:36,865 --> 00:11:38,995
that contains a
target sub directory.

263
00:11:38,995 --> 00:11:40,705
Inside that target directory

264
00:11:40,705 --> 00:11:42,070
you see this message that says,

265
00:11:42,070 --> 00:11:43,960
"This is not your
root file system."

266
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:46,330
What this is telling you is that

267
00:11:46,330 --> 00:11:51,415
your root file system is not
an exact replica of this.

268
00:11:51,415 --> 00:11:54,235
By manipulating this,
you aren't necessarily

269
00:11:54,235 --> 00:11:55,885
manipulating things
like permissions

270
00:11:55,885 --> 00:11:57,895
of the content of your
root file system.

271
00:11:57,895 --> 00:12:00,085
However, this does contain

272
00:12:00,085 --> 00:12:01,360
the content that's used to build

273
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:02,935
your root file system image.

274
00:12:02,935 --> 00:12:05,440
You can go in here and manually

275
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:08,110
remove packages if you have

276
00:12:08,110 --> 00:12:10,450
removed them from your
image and they will be

277
00:12:10,450 --> 00:12:13,150
reflected in the image
that shows up ultimately.

278
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:15,070
You can sometimes use this as

279
00:12:15,070 --> 00:12:17,185
a shortcut so that you
don't have to clean build.

280
00:12:17,185 --> 00:12:19,405
It's also a good
troubleshooting step.

281
00:12:19,405 --> 00:12:21,490
If you know that you're

282
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:23,665
missing a file in your
image for some reason,

283
00:12:23,665 --> 00:12:26,290
rather than having to completely

284
00:12:26,290 --> 00:12:28,540
build the image and then
boot it up and QEMU,

285
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:30,550
you can just run your
package Build and

286
00:12:30,550 --> 00:12:32,695
look to see if it's been
installed in this directory.

287
00:12:32,695 --> 00:12:34,960
If it hasn't been, then you
know it's not going to be

288
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:38,360
there when you complete
the imaging step as well.